/-!
# Descent to integer exponents in the Hahn field K((t^ℚ))

Theorem statement: `HahnSeries.hasRamBound_one_of_forall_ringEquiv_apply_eq`
(`4b526abf-db34-59d8-87de-575bb8c429d0`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `457d8709-8454-5e41-8f06-d14bae3b65be`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ be a field that is algebraically closed and of characteristic zero, and consider the Hahn
series field $\mathrm{HahnSeries}\ \mathbb Q\ K$, whose elements are formal series with coefficients
in $K$ supported on a well-ordered set of rational exponents. For $e : \mathbb N$ and a Hahn series
$y$, the predicate [`HahnSeries.HasRamBound e y`](def/HahnSeries_RamificationBound.html#L32) asserts
that the support of $y$ is contained in the set of rationals of the form $k/e$ with $k \in \mathbb
Z$; thus [`HahnSeries.HasRamBound 1 y`](def/HahnSeries_RamificationBound.html#L32) says exactly that
every exponent occurring in $y$ is an integer, i.e. that $y$ lies in the Laurent subfield $K((t))
\subseteq K((t^{\mathbb Q}))$. Let $x$ be a Hahn series with rational exponents over $K$, and assume
that for every ring isomorphism $\sigma$ of $\mathrm{HahnSeries}\ \mathbb Q\ K$ onto itself which
preserves `orderTop`, in the sense that $(\sigma z).\mathrm{orderTop} = z.\mathrm{orderTop}$ for all
$z$, and which fixes every series all of whose exponents are integers, one has $\sigma x = x$. The
conclusion is that the support of $x$ consists of integers, i.e. [`HahnSeries.HasRamBound 1
x`](def/HahnSeries_RamificationBound.html#L32) holds.

This is the elementwise form of the descent statement that the fixed field of the
`orderTop`-preserving automorphisms of $K((t^{\mathbb Q}))$ fixing $K((t))$ pointwise is $K((t))$
itself, the Galois-theoretic half of the Puiseux tower $K((t)) \subset K((t^{1/e})) \subset
K((t^{\mathbb Q}))$; note that the automorphisms quantified over are merely ring isomorphisms, not
assumed $K$-linear. It is used to bound ramification of Puiseux expansions attached to torsion
points of a Weierstrass curve over a power series ring, in
[`WeierstrassCurve.hasRamBound_one_of_nsmul_eq_zero_of_isUnit_discriminant_powerSeries`]
(thm.html#WeierstrassCurve.hasRamBound_one_of_nsmul_eq_zero_of_isUnit_discriminant_powerSeries).

